Especially knowledge that teaches you what to notice. Once you learn to hear the premise tucked inside “Why are you so defensive?”, you start hearing loaded premises in conversations you had years ago. The words you remember can stay the same while your reading of them changes. Then you point out the pattern to somebody else, and they start finding examples you’ve never heard.

The virus analogy goes one step deeper. The original wording can disappear while the pattern keeps replicating. Somebody reads Silent Guitar and forgets the name. Weeks later they leave a deliberate gap in a story so a kid can invent the next line. The kid tries it on a friend. What traveled was a way of making room for somebody else to play.

That’s a useful distinction for Plat Forming Plat. One branch gives you a new answer. Another gives you a way to find new answers on problems the original author never saw. That second kind can keep forming platforms long after the first one is gone.

And a bad idea can spread the same way. Once a lens tells you what to notice, it can also tell you what to overlook. So the version I’d keep in the Money Bin carries the pattern, an example where it works, and an example where it breaks. That way the next person inherits enough to revise it.